From ce4cd320f6d8c285c9f2e1a8040fb7af83c0b821 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 10:19:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] qos: preset rules no longer classify by transferred bytes

Backlog [P2], found on live metal 2026-08-10. The shipped qos_rulelist was

  Web Surf>>80>tcp>0~512>0   HTTPS>>443>tcp>0~512>0
  File Transfer>>80>tcp>512~>3   File Transfer>>443>tcp>512~>3

so any HTTP/HTTPS flow past 512 KB was demoted to the bulk class. Streaming
video IS HTTPS and passes that in seconds, so a TV's flows -- and under type 11,
which shapes UPLOAD, its ACK stream -- rode the bulk queue while the queue named
"Streaming" showed 0 packets. Worse, a rule carrying a transferred range gets
rule_gum = 0 in add_qos_rules() (non-sticky) and matches with -m connbytes,
which re-evaluates on every packet: incompatible with hardware flow offload. The
standing guidance since the v2 metal validation has been "classify on
port/proto/IP/MAC, never transferred bytes" -- the shipped defaults were the
thing violating it.

NEW PACK (every rule has an EMPTY transferred field, so all are sticky and
offload-safe). First match wins -- add_qos_rules emits CONNMARK --set-return, or
--set-mark plus an explicit RETURN -- so specific precedes broad:

  Conferencing  udp 3478:3481    -> Q1 Web/VoIP   (STUN/TURN: Teams, Meet, WebRTC)
  SIP           udp 5060:5061    -> Q1 Web/VoIP
  Game Console  udp 3074         -> Q2 Gaming     (Xbox Live / PSN)
  Steam         udp 27015:27030  -> Q2 Gaming
  BitTorrent    tcp/udp 6881:6889-> Q4 Downloads  (default-port clients only)
  Web           tcp/udp 80,443   -> Q1 Web/VoIP

Two deliberate choices worth stating. There is NO Streaming preset: streaming is
TCP/UDP 443, indistinguishable from browsing by port, which is precisely why the
old pack reached for byte counts -- that class is meant to be filled by
per-device rules (the TV's MAC/IP), which are sticky. And 80/443 sits in
Web/VoIP rather than anywhere lower, because it carries interactive web AND the
ACK stream of every download, both of which want a short queue; the committed
minimums on the classes below (qos_minrates) stop a bulk HTTPS upload in that
class from starving them. Ports are tcp/udp so QUIC gets the same treatment as
TCP for the same traffic.

Validated against what the parser actually accepts, not by eye: 6 fields per
rule, prio 0-4, port charset inside the multiport injection guard, known proto,
empty transferred, multiport entry budget (a range counts as 2 of 15).

WARNING IN THE UI: Reaper_QoS.asp now reads qos_rulelist and, under the classful
engine only, names any rule still carrying a transferred range (the stock editor
writes a bare "~" for "no range" -- treated as empty). Rules are still edited on
the stock user-rules page; this page only reports. Exercised by running the
page's own rulesWarn() against the old pack (warns, names all four), the new
pack (silent), a bare "~" (silent), an empty list (silent), and the old pack in
a non-classful mode (silent).

New token RQOS_123, English-seeded, appended to all 25 dicts in lockstep
(6294 -> 6295 lines each); translation pass owed with the RFWU_* backlog item.

NOT BUILT. Metal owed: confirm the presets land as written after a factory
reset, and that a Teams/Meet call now marks into Q1 (it will NOT when the call
is inside a VPN -- the tunnel hides the ports and it correctly falls to Default;
that case still wants a per-endpoint rule).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
